From 15491cb03190e5a5e644c249f1c5cceda7f68b54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 10:49:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/16] hw/sd: model a generic SDIO function

QEMU implements SD and eMMC memory cards and no SDIO at all, so a guest
driver for the SDIO transport -- CMD52 and CMD53, which is how a
Raspberry Pi reaches its Wi-Fi part -- cannot be run at all.

Add an sd-io device: an I/O-only function with the card common control
registers at function 0, one I/O function with a register space behind
it, and the SDIO command set.  It answers CMD5, CMD3, CMD7, CMD52 and
CMD53.  It deliberately answers nothing else: CMD8, ACMD41 and CMD9 are
absent from its protocol table, so a driver that probes with the memory
card sequence is told the command is unsupported rather than being
quietly indulged.  Register semantics are from the SDIO simplified
specification.

Protocol violations are reported rather than tolerated -- a register
access outside the function's address space, a CMD53 to a function that
has not been enabled and reported ready, a transfer longer than the
model moves in one command, a voltage window it cannot supply.

An I/O-only function has no CID, no CSD and no OCR in the memory sense,
so it takes neither the card register setup in sd_reset -- which would
call class hooks it does not provide -- nor a drive, and it reports
itself always present since it is soldered down rather than in a slot.

Also add sdbus_is_occupied() and sdbus_holds_card(), so a board can ask
whether a bus is free and whether what is on it is a removable card
rather than a soldered part.  An SD bus takes one device, and pin
routing moves the card slot, not things wired to one controller.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
